# Sanitize gate host allowlist (scripts/sanitize-check.sh, rule R1)
#
# One host per line. Comment lines (starting with #) and blank lines are ignored.
# Any host ending in "example.com" is allowed automatically and does not need
# to be listed here. Everything else found in a workflow export must either be
# listed below (a legitimate third-party API this project actually calls) or
# it fails the gate.

# HubSpot
api.hubapi.com
app.hubspot.com

# Salesforce
login.salesforce.com
test.salesforce.com
# Org-specific REST/Bulk API hosts (e.g. na139.my.salesforce.com,
# <org>.lightning.force.com) are allowed by a wildcard pattern in
# scripts/sanitize-check.sh (hostAllowed), not listed here. The instance
# subdomain isn't sensitive on a free Dev Edition org, but adding one org's
# literal hostname to this tracked file would accumulate real org
# identifiers over time.

# Anthropic (Claude API)
api.anthropic.com

# Cal.com
api.cal.com

# Slack
slack.com
hooks.slack.com

# Mailtrap
send.api.mailtrap.io
sandbox.api.mailtrap.io

# Clay
api.clay.com

# OpenAI
api.openai.com

# Instantly
api.instantly.ai

# Apollo is deliberately absent. Case 5 sources its prospect list from a
# saved-search CSV export, not the API, so no workflow calls an Apollo host.
# An allowlist entry for a host the project no longer calls would let one
# reappear in an export without the gate saying anything.

# Local development
localhost
# Case 1's Docker-compose mock enrichment service (simulates a third-party
# enrichment vendor for the graceful-degradation scenario). Internal-only
# hostname, no external DNS record, no credential attached.
enrichment-mock

sheets.googleapis.com
